{{- $submit := Default "Commit" .Args.SubmitStrategy }}
{{- $maxWorkers := Default "1" .Args.MaxParallelWorkers }}
{{- if eq $submit "Pull Request" }}{{ $maxWorkers = "1" }}{{ end }}
{{- $hookArgs := ArgsMap "PostIterationPrompt_Args" -}}
## Step 5H — Post-task hook (after ONE task completes, before the next is picked)

Reached **only** from the completion branches above — §A "Driver done", §B/§C
"Bead closed" — i.e. a real task just finished. Do NOT run it on the at-cap,
nothing-eligible, timeout, wait-errored or Step 7 (deferral) paths: this hook
marks *a task finished*, not *a pass finished*.

**Exclusivity gate (mandatory, run BEFORE the spawn).** The hook needs the working
tree to itself: it must not start while any other worker is running, and while it
runs no other worker may start. This is **exclusivity, not a slot count** —{{ if ne $maxWorkers "1" }} even with
the cap raised to {{ $maxWorkers }}, the hook requires a live worker count of exactly 0.{{ else }} it holds
the single workspace worker slot for its whole run.{{ end }} The
pre-rendered `Workspace peers:` block is a template-dispatch-time snapshot and
is stale by now (a driver may have been spawned earlier in this very pass), so
re-enumerate live:

```
mitto_conversation_list(
  self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}",
  archived: false,
  working_dir: "{{ .Workspace.Folder }}"
)
```

Count entries whose `title` starts with `Fix `, `Implement ` or `Post-task: `
AND which also have a non-empty `beads_issue` field (`ConversationInfo.BeadsIssue`
from this live list) — a bare title-prefix match on a conversation with no
linked bead does NOT count (mitto-e1x). Exclude this session itself and the
just-finished child you archived in §A/§B/§C above (it may still appear if the
archive has not settled — match it by `conversation_id`).

- Count ≥ 1 → **do not spawn**. Write
  `bd comment <id> "Orchestrator: post-task hook ({{ .Args.PostIterationPrompt }}) skipped — another worker is active in the workspace; will run after a later task completes."`
  and jump to Step 6 with hook status `not-run`. Never queue, never retry
  inline: skipping is correct, the hook fires after the next completed task.
- Count = 0 → proceed with the spawn below.

Dispatch the operator-picked follow-up as its **own child conversation** so its
token spend, file reads and tool calls never pollute this supervisor's context,
then block until it reports. Spawn it **by name** (`prompt_name`, never
`initial_prompt`) — the server expands `{{ .Args.PostIterationPrompt }}` in the
child's own context, exactly like the §A/§B/§C drivers (mitto-dj9); you never
inline or execute the hook's text yourself. The `Post-task: ` title prefix is
load-bearing: it is what §B/§C's concurrency gate counts, so peer orchestrators
hold off while this hook runs. `<id>` is the bead just completed (for §A, the
bead carrying the addressed mention):

{{- $hookArgs := ArgsMap "PostIterationPrompt_Args" }}

```
mitto_conversation_new(
  self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}",
  title: "Post-task: <id>",
  beads_issue: "<id>",
  prompt_name: "{{ .Args.PostIterationPrompt }}"{{ if $hookArgs }},
  arguments: { {{ $sep := "" }}{{ range $k, $v := $hookArgs }}{{ $sep }}"{{ $k }}": "{{ $v }}"{{ $sep = ", " }}{{ end }} }{{ end }}
)
```

Record the returned `conversation_id` as **`<hook-child-id>`**, then block on it,
passing the context/reporting inquiry as the wait's own prompt (the wait appends
the reporting instructions for you):

```
mitto_children_tasks_wait(
  self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}",
  children_list: ["<hook-child-id>"],
  task_id: "post-task-<id>",
  prompt: "You ran as a post-task hook right after beads issue <id> was completed by the Loop processing tasks orchestrator, before the next task is picked. Summarize the outcome.",
  timeout_seconds: 3600
)
```

- **Reported** — log and delete the hook child:
  `bd comment <id> "Orchestrator: post-task hook ({{ .Args.PostIterationPrompt }}) completed: <one-line outcome from the report>."`
  then `mitto_conversation_delete(self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}", conversation_id: "<hook-child-id>")`.
- **Timed out (1h) or no report** — the hook holds the workspace worker slot, so
  it must not be left running indefinitely:
  `bd comment <id> "Orchestrator: post-task hook ({{ .Args.PostIterationPrompt }}) did not report within 1h; deleting hook child to release the worker slot."`
  then `mitto_conversation_delete(self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}", conversation_id: "<hook-child-id>")`
  and continue.
- **Spawn failed** (flag off, quota, unknown prompt) — one
  `bd comment <id> "Orchestrator: post-task hook ({{ .Args.PostIterationPrompt }}) could not be dispatched: <error>; skipped."` and continue.
  Do NOT run the hook inline — the supervisor stays procedural.

Best-effort by construction: a hook that fails, times out or reports a problem
**never** blocks the next task and never re-opens the completed bead. If the hook
discovers real work (e.g. tests it ran now fail), it is the hook prompt's own job
to file a bead — the orchestrator will pick it up on the next `onTasks` pass.

The hook child **does** count against the workspace worker cap while it runs
(that mutual exclusion is the point: a `Run tests`-style hook must never race a
driver editing the same tree). Every exit branch above therefore releases the
slot — reported and timed-out both end in `mitto_conversation_delete`. Step 2P
still never reaps a `Post-task: …` child: Step 5H owns its lifecycle.

Then Step 6.

